<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer" />
    <title>{@page_title}</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href={~p"/assets/app.css"} />
  </head>

  <%!-- Deliberately script-free: this page only describes what the app does. Keep it in sync with docs/security.md. --%>
  <body class="doc-page">
    <main class="doc" id="security">
      <header class="doc-header">
        <img src={~p"/images/marknotes-mark.svg"} width="40" height="40" alt="" />
        <div>
          <h1>How MarkNotes protects your notes</h1>

          <p class="doc-lead">
            MarkNotes runs in your browser. The MarkNotes server delivers the app and never receives your notes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="server">
        <h2 id="server">What the server sees</h2>

        <p>
          The server sends the app's code, a health check and the page that completes Dropbox or Microsoft sign-in.
          It has no database and no accounts. Note content, note and folder names, images, drive tokens and edits are
          never sent to it, and it cannot read them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Editing, previewing, storing and syncing all happen in your browser. Sync talks directly to your drive
          provider.
        </p>

        <p>
          The sign-in page contains no request data, and authorization codes are filtered from server logs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="storage">
        <h2 id="storage">Where your notes are kept</h2>

        <p>
          You choose how this browser keeps notes in <strong>Settings › Privacy and security</strong>:
        </p>

        <dl class="doc-modes">
          <dt>In this browser (default)</dt>

          <dd>
            Notes, images and sync state are stored in this browser's IndexedDB storage. They stay there after the tab
            closes, so anyone who can use this browser profile, or copy its files, can read them. Drive tokens are
            encrypted with a key the browser will not export, but that key is stored in the same place. This stops
            tokens being read straight from storage files. It does not stop someone who can run code in this browser.
          </dd>

          <dt>Encrypted with a passphrase</dt>

          <dd>
            <p>
              Everything MarkNotes stores is encrypted: notes, names, images, sync state and drive tokens.
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>
                Your passphrase unlocks a random 256-bit key, derived with PBKDF2-SHA-256 and 600,000 iterations.
              </li>

              <li>Each record is sealed with AES-256-GCM.</li>

              <li>
                Record names are replaced with HMAC-SHA-256 identifiers, so note and folder names are not visible on disk.
              </li>

              <li>Altered or swapped records are detected and refused.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              While unlocked, your notes are decrypted in this tab's memory only. MarkNotes locks itself after 15
              minutes without activity (5, 60 or off in Settings), and you can lock it at any time. The passphrase cannot be recovered.
            </p>
          </dd>

          <dt>Memory only</dt>

          <dd>
            Nothing is written to this browser's storage. Notes, images and drive connections exist only while this tab
            is open; closing or reloading it forgets them. Your synced notes stay on your drives. Use this on shared
            or borrowed computers.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <p>
          <strong>Forget this device</strong>
          deletes everything MarkNotes stored in this browser: notes, images,
          sync state, drive connections, passphrase data and settings. Your drives are not touched. It is available in
          Settings, and on the unlock screen if you have forgotten your passphrase.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="network">
        <h2 id="network">What the app may connect to</h2>

        <p>
          A Content Security Policy blocks every other site. The app may only connect to:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>the MarkNotes server;</li>

          <li>the Dropbox API;</li>

          <li>Microsoft Graph and Microsoft sign-in;</li>

          <li>Microsoft's OneDrive download hosts.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          OneDrive file downloads use pre-signed addresses, which receive no Microsoft token. MarkNotes refuses
          addresses outside Microsoft's content hosts.
        </p>

        <p>
          The app loads no third-party scripts, analytics or fonts. The markdown preview never loads remote images or
          runs raw HTML: it only shows images from your own <code>attachments/</code>
          folder. Pages are not cached and
          send no referrer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="why">
        <h2 id="why">Why notes are not kept on the server</h2>

        <p>
          A server-side copy, even one held only in memory, would let the server, and anyone who compromised it, read
          every user's notes and drive tokens at once. Keeping notes in your browser means a server breach exposes
          none of them.
        </p>

        <p>
          The cost is that your browser has to protect them. Passphrase encryption and memory-only mode exist for that
          reason.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="limits">
        <h2 id="limits">What this does not protect against</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            Malware, malicious browser extensions or someone using this browser while MarkNotes is unlocked: they see
            what you see.
          </li>

          <li>
            A flaw that lets an attacker run script inside the MarkNotes page would also see your unlocked notes. The
            Content Security Policy and preview sanitiser are there to prevent this.
          </li>

          <li>
            Your drive provider can read the notes you sync. MarkNotes does not encrypt files on your drives, so the
            Android and desktop apps can read the same folder.
          </li>

          <li>
            With a passphrase, the number and approximate size of stored records are still visible on disk, but not
            their content or names.
          </li>

          <li>
            Notes stored before you set a passphrase are deleted when you set one. The browser may still keep old copies
            in its storage files until it compacts them; for the strongest protection, set a passphrase or use memory
            only before writing sensitive notes.
          </li>

          <li>
            A forgotten passphrase cannot be reset. Forget this device, then sync your notes again from your drives;
            anything not yet synced is lost.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <footer class="doc-footer">
        <p>
          Found a security problem? Please report it privately through <a
            href="https://github.com/MaxDac/marknotes-web/security/advisories/new"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            GitHub security advisories
          </a>.
        </p>

        <p><a href="/">Back to MarkNotes</a></p>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
